# WTDRV2M.KEY - the mouse lines of the driver, run as WTDRV2 /MOUSE:
# pads A (70, CS_DBLCLKS) holding C (70.1) and B (71) report each
# message they get, the main window those it gets while it holds the
# mouse, in the status line; TEST/mousechk.py replays the script on a
# model of the pads and checks every snapshot.
S INIT
# A move with no button held, at a client point, then a press at the
# middle, held: the pad holds the mouse.
M 70 MOVE AT 5 7
S MOVE
M 70 DOWN MID
S DOWN
M 70 MOVE MID -10 3
M 70 UP AT 2 -3
S UP
# Pressed on B, then named at A: the holder gets the move and the
# release, at A's points in its own client area; a key typed meanwhile
# leaves the button held.
M 71 DOWN AT 10 10
K SHIFT-A
M 70 MOVE AT 5 5
S ROUTED
M 70 UP AT 6 6
S ROUTEUP
# A path two deep: C inside A.
M 70.1 DOWN AT 3 4
S NESTED
M 70.1 UP AT 3 4
# Double clicks: A's class takes them, B's gets a press.
M 70 DOWN AT 1 1
M 70 UP AT 1 1
M 70 DBL AT 1 1
S DBLA
M 70 UP AT 1 1
M 71 DOWN AT 1 1
M 71 UP AT 1 1
M 71 DBL AT 1 1
S DBLB
M 71 UP AT 1 1
# A right press gives the mouse to the main window, a top-level window:
# no CAPTURE; a move and the release named at B go to it.
M 70 RDOWN AT 8 9
S RDOWN
M 71 MOVE AT 2 3
M 71 RUP AT 2 3
S RUP
# Both buttons held, then released in turn.
M 71 DOWN AT 4 4
M 71 RDOWN AT 4 4
M 70 RUP AT 4 4
M 71 UP AT 4 4
S BOTH
# Right double clicks: A's class takes it, B's gets a press.
M 70 RDBL AT 2 2
M 70 RUP AT 2 2
M 71 RDBL AT 2 2
M 71 RUP AT 2 2
S RDBL
# A row of the list box: pressed, the box holds the mouse; released.
M 20 DOWN ROW 2
S ROWDN
M 20 UP ROW 2
S ROWUP
